Doug Freyburger <dfreybur@yahoo.com> wrote:
>
> I have two different answers, neither absolute -

I have two replies, neither absolute either, one more on technical
side, the other more on the design side. :-)

> 1) On the sourceforge web site it says Nethack is a mature project.  To
> anyone who has taken software engineering courses at a university it
> means something specific when the maintainer is the one who makes that
> declaration.

But "mature" doesn't mean "bugfree".

Usually and IMO also in the case of NetHack 3.4.3 it means "old code,
long time in use, people know the behavior of the program, including
the bugs and how to workaround them".


There are some nasty crash bugs in vanilla NetHack 3.4.3. For example
long worms are a regular source of crashes (although I don't know if
the DevTeam tamed that little crash bugger).

There are also bugs that may happen without the player being at fault.

The lovely crash when a monster with a potion of acid stands on a
drawbridge, this drawbridge collapses because of another monster
zapping a wand of striking, the potion of acid and the moat react with
an explosion and then the game might crash because of a dangling
pointer as it tries to put the destroyed potion onto the bottom of the
moat.

Even when we disregard the discussion if NetHack needs new features or
lacks anything, the technical answer for a new release is "yes, even
if only for bugfixes".

> 2) If you graph the historical dates it fits a software lifecycle curve.
> Since the release of  3.1.0 the history was earlier in the curve.

Games don't necessarily follow that curve closely as Game design
dictates different requirements than specifying and implementing
normal applications do.

If the DevTeam stopped releasing NetHack after 3.0.10, we would have
lead the exact same discussion. But with 3.1.0 they introduced a
branched dungeon that pumped new life into NetHack (and a whole lot of
new bugs than wanted to be squashed).

You make it sound like the DevTeam had a vision of the one and only
true NetHack and 3.4.3 is the culmination of that.

But 3.4.3 surely isn't perfect and there have been countless threads
in this group that discussed its design deficiencies.

We don't know where the DevTeam wants to take NetHack but we've seen
the variants. Slash'Em and SporkHack certainly went off into a
different direction than the DevTeam, I don't know how far off I am
with UnNetHack, but 3.4.3 is certainly not the end and pinnacle of
NetHack's game design.

The patches I applied and changes I made in the first few weeks of the
UnNetHack development added about 5% LOC to the existing code base
and those were rather uncontroversial patches that have been used since
years.

That's a significant amount and I was surprised how much it was. But I
shouldn't have been that surprised as NetHack has a rather active
community that likes to write patches and with no public visible
maintainer, where should those patches go?

From a design POV there is still a lot that can be done with NetHack's
code that would justify a new release.

As a variant developer I'm of course also interested in seeing what
DevTeam did different than Derek or I or if they only added
mostly superficially features as they did in 3.4.0.

